\name{sdrf_parse_ages}
\alias{sdrf_parse_ages}
\title{Read SDRF age cells into years, refusing anything that would need a guess}
\usage{
sdrf_parse_ages(cells, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{cells}{A character vector of cells, e.g.
  the age characteristic's values from \code{\link{sdrf_value}}. \code{NA} is sent as an empty cell and comes back
  refused as \code{"empty"}, so the result stays aligned with the input.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Read characteristics[age] cells into years with an honest precision, refusing any cell that would need a guess.

Calls mzLib's \code{SdrfAge.TryParse} on each cell. It reads the specification's grammar (\code{58Y},
\code{30Y6M}, \code{16W}), ranges (\code{40Y-85Y}, \code{6-8 weeks}), bounds (\code{>=90Y}, \code{<1Y}) and unambiguous words
(\code{3 year}, \code{4 hour}). It \strong{refuses} a bare number - \code{63} is 11\% of real age cells, and 63 years
and 63 days are both plausible in one study - as well as reserved words and free text.
}
\details{
One bridge call for any number of cells, so pass them all at once rather than looping.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_sdrf_ages}: \code{cell_count} cells given, \code{parsed_count} cells read as an age,
  \code{caveats}, \code{column_names}, and \code{records}, one row per cell \strong{in input order}: \code{cell}, as
  given; \code{years}, the single figure to place the sample on an age axis, in years - the age, a
  range's midpoint, or a bound; \code{min_years}, the youngest the cell allows, in years (0 for an
  upper bound); \code{max_years}, the oldest, in years; \code{precision} (\code{"Exact"}, \code{"Range"},
  \code{"LowerBound"} or \code{"UpperBound"}); \code{follows_specification} (\code{TRUE} for the specification's own
  \code{nYnMnD} / \code{nW} grammar); and \code{refusal} - \code{NA} when the cell was read, else \code{"empty"},
  \code{"reserved_word"}, \code{"no_unit"} or \code{"unreadable"}.

  \code{years}, \code{min_years}, \code{max_years}, \code{precision} and \code{follows_specification} are \code{NA} for a
  refused cell. \code{max_years} is also \code{NA} for a lower bound such as \code{>=90Y}, which has no upper
  limit; \code{precision} tells the two apart. A month is 1/12 year, a week 7/365.25, a day 1/365.25.

}
